.TH TTY 1
.SH NAME
tty \- get terminal name
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tty
[
.B \-s
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Tty
prints the pathname of the user's terminal \- the name in
.B /dev
of whatever is open on the standard input.
.PP
.B \-s
prints nothing; the answer is the exit status alone, which is what a shell script
wants.
.B Note:
v7's page documented neither the option nor the status, although the program has always
had both.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
`not a tty' if the standard input file is not a terminal.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
0 if the standard input is a terminal, 1 if it is not.
.SH FILES
.ta \w'/dev  'u
/dev	searched for the name
.SH "SEE ALSO"
ttyname(3)
.SH BUGS
There is no way back from a device number to a name, so
.IR ttyname (3)
reads
.B /dev
and compares i-numbers.  A terminal that is not in
.B /dev
has no name, and one that is there twice answers with whichever entry comes first in the
directory.
